1. Long-Term Scheduler

Also called:

Job Scheduler
Main Work

Decides:

Which programs are allowed to enter RAM?

Remember:

SSD may contain:

Chrome
VS Code
Spotify
Photoshop
Steam
Games
100 more applications

But RAM is limited.

Example:

RAM = 8 GB

All programs cannot be loaded.

So OS decides:

Load Chrome
Load VS Code
Load Spotify

Don't load Game yet

This decision is made by:

Long-Term Scheduler
Real-Life Example

Imagine a college.

10,000 students apply.

College can admit only 500.

Admission committee decides who enters.

Admission Committee = Long-Term Scheduler

Process State Movement
New
 ↓
Ready

Long-Term Scheduler moves processes from:

New → Ready
Example

You click:

Chrome
Spotify
VS Code

OS creates processes.

Long-Term Scheduler decides:

Chrome → Ready
Spotify → Ready
VS Code → Ready
Main Goal

Control:

Degree of Multiprogramming
What is Degree of Multiprogramming?

Number of processes in memory.

Example:

10 processes in RAM

Degree of Multiprogramming = 10

Long-Term Scheduler controls this number.

Frequency

Runs rarely.

Why?

Because processes don't enter memory every millisecond.

Interview Point
Long-Term Scheduler

Selects processes from secondary storage and loads them into memory.

2. Short-Term Scheduler

Most Important Scheduler.

Also called:

CPU Scheduler
Main Work

Decides:

Which Ready Process gets CPU?

Suppose Ready Queue:

Chrome
VS Code
Spotify
WhatsApp

Only one CPU core available.

Who gets CPU?

Short-Term Scheduler decides.

Example

Ready Queue:

Chrome
VS Code
Spotify

Scheduler decides:

Chrome → Running

After a few milliseconds:

VS Code → Running

Then:

Spotify → Running

This switching happens thousands of times per second.

Real-Life Example

Doctor's clinic.

Waiting room:

Patient A
Patient B
Patient C

Doctor chooses next patient.

Doctor = Short-Term Scheduler

State Transition

Moves process:

Ready → Running

and sometimes:

Running → Ready
Frequency

Runs extremely often.

Sometimes every few milliseconds.

Interview Point
Short-Term Scheduler

Selects a process from the Ready Queue and allocates CPU.

3. Medium-Term Scheduler

Most students ignore this one.

Interviewers love asking it.

Main Work

Temporarily removes processes from RAM.

This is called:

Swapping
Why Needed?

Suppose:

RAM = 8 GB

Processes need:

Chrome = 2 GB
VS Code = 1 GB
Game = 5 GB
Spotify = 1 GB

Total:

9 GB

Problem:

RAM only 8 GB.

OS may decide:

Move Spotify out temporarily

to SSD.

This is:

Swap Out

Later:

Bring Spotify back

This is:

Swap In

Medium-Term Scheduler manages this.

Real-Life Example

Hostel.

Rooms are full.

Warden temporarily shifts some students elsewhere.

When space becomes available:

Students return.

Warden = Medium-Term Scheduler

State Transition

Usually:

Ready ↔ Suspended
Running ↔ Suspended
Frequency

Runs occasionally.

More frequent than Long-Term.

Less frequent than Short-Term.

# Comparison Table
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Feature      | Long-Term        | Medium-Term              | Short-Term      |
| ------------ | ---------------- | ------------------------ | --------------- |
| Also Called  | Job Scheduler    | Swapper                  | CPU Scheduler   |
| Main Work    | Admit Processes  | Remove/Restore Processes | Allocate CPU    |
| Works On     | New Processes    | Processes in Memory      | Ready Queue     |
| Frequency    | Rare             | Occasional               | Very Frequent   |
| Controls     | Multiprogramming | Memory Pressure          | CPU Usage       |
| State Change | New → Ready      | Ready ↔ Suspended        | Ready → Running |
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Complete Story Using Chrome

Suppose laptop starts.

Step 1

You open Chrome.

Process created.

State:

New
Step 2

Long-Term Scheduler says:

Allow Chrome into RAM

State:

Ready
Step 3

Short-Term Scheduler says:

Chrome gets CPU

State:

Running
Step 4

RAM becomes full.

Medium-Term Scheduler says:

Move Spotify out temporarily

State:

Suspended
Step 5

Memory available again.

Medium-Term Scheduler says:

Bring Spotify back

State:

Ready
